Acinar cells produce the two main types of saliva: serous and mucinous. Serous saliva contains the enzyme amylase responsible for the initial breakdown of complex carbohydrates and is predominantly produced by the parotid gland, the largest of salivary glands. The mucinous type, more common in the submandibular and sublingual glands, contains mucin that aids in lubrication, facilitating the process of mastication and bolus formation.
