The primary function of the vasa recta is to participate in the countercurrent exchange system, a crucial mechanism for establishing and maintaining the concentration gradient in the kidney&apos;s medulla. This gradient is essential for the process of urine concentration. The vasa recta runs parallel to the nephron&apos;s loop of Henle, and as blood flows through these vessels, it undergoes exchange with the surrounding interstitial fluid. This exchange allows for the reabsorption of water and solutes, helping to preserve the hypertonic environment of the medulla and facilitating the production of concentrated urine.

